{"duration_api_ms":493970,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"7368dc68-0e9b-4ddb-b6ba-e7c137ebf9e9","total_cost_usd":2.3721356,"usage":{"input_tokens":204,"cache_creation_input_tokens":92369,"cache_read_input_tokens":7943023,"output_tokens":40947,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":8587},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":92369,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3213,"cache_read_input_tokens":108130,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1263,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1263},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":4072,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004177,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":204,"outputTokens":40947,"cacheReadInputTokens":7943023,"cacheCreationInputTokens":92369,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.3679586,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":8587,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":129,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":\"Deployed a 3-node RabbitMQ 4.2.7 cluster (node1/node2/node3, Alpine 3.23) serving AMQP exclusively over TLS on port 5671, secured by a purpose-built CA. Client certificate authentication is mandatory (verify_peer + fail_if_no_peer_cert=true); the plaintext 5672 listener is disabled entirely. The management UI (15672) is gated by an iptables chain (RMQ_MGMT) that only accepts traffic from the three cluster node IPs and drops everything else, persisted via rc-update/iptables-save. Ran the mandatory one-at-a-time restart protocol on all three nodes: captured baseline boot_id, rebooted, confirmed boot_id changed, and verified RabbitMQ/TLS/firewall/cluster membership fully recovered before moving to the next node, preserving quorum throughout. Along the way discovered and fixed a persistence bug: /etc/hostname was \\\"localhost\\\" (runtime hostname set out-of-band by provisioning), which caused node3's first reboot to rejoin the cluster under a spurious Erlang node name; fixed /etc/hostname on all three nodes and cleaned up the stray cluster member before node2/node1 restarts, after which both came back correctly as rabbit@node2/rabbit@node1.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Installed rabbitmq-server, erlang27, openssl, iptables on all three nodes via apk\",\"Generated a dedicated CA (ca_key.pem/ca_cert.pem) on node1\",\"Issued CA-signed server certs (with SAN DNS+IP entries for all 3 nodes) for node1/node2/node3 and a CA-signed client cert for connection testing; verified full chain of trust with openssl verify\",\"Distributed CA cert plus per-node server cert/key to /etc/rabbitmq/tls on each node (via a temporary SSH unlock of root, then relocked root accounts afterward) with rabbitmq:rabbitmq ownership and restrictive file permissions\",\"Generated one shared Erlang cookie on node1 and distributed it identically (md5-verified) to node2/node3\",\"Wrote rabbitmq.conf on all nodes: listeners.tcp = none (disables plaintext AMQP), listeners.ssl.default = 5671, ssl_options with cacertfile/certfile/keyfile, verify = verify_peer, fail_if_no_peer_cert = true, classic_config peer discovery listing all three nodes, cluster_partition_handling = pause_minority\",\"Created and persisted (iptables-save + rc-update add iptables default) an RMQ_MGMT iptables chain on every node restricting TCP/15672 to the three cluster node IPs and dropping all other sources\",\"Started rabbitmq-server on all three nodes and confirmed automatic cluster formation (Running Nodes: rabbit@node1/2/3, no partitions)\",\"Enabled rabbitmq_management plugin on all nodes; created an administrator user (rmqadmin, generated password) and deleted the default guest account\",\"Enabled rabbitmq-server and iptables in the default OpenRC runlevel on all nodes so both survive reboot\",\"Verified TLS enforcement: openssl s_client and a Python/pika AMQP publish+consume test succeed only when a CA-issued client cert is presented; both fail with 'certificate required' TLS alert otherwise\",\"Verified management firewall: curl to the management API succeeds (200 OK, authenticated) from a cluster node IP and is dropped/times out from a non-listed source (127.0.0.1)\",\"Discovered /etc/hostname was 'localhost' on all nodes (runtime hostname set out-of-band); after node3's first reboot it rejoined as rabbit@vm-rhel8-... instead of rabbit@node3 — fixed /etc/hostname on all three nodes, forgot the stray cluster member, and cleaned up its mnesia state\",\"Executed the mandatory one-node-at-a-time restart protocol on node3, node2, then node1: captured pre-restart boot_id via /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id, issued a background 'reboot' whose acknowledgement returned before disconnect, polled until reachable, confirmed boot_id changed, and confirmed RabbitMQ cluster membership, TLS listener, and management firewall all recovered before proceeding to the next node\",\"Final full-cluster verification after all three restarts: cluster_status shows all three nodes running with no partitions, TLS client-cert enforcement still works end-to-end (publish/consume succeeds with cert, fails without), management API reachable/authenticated from cluster IP and blocked from a non-cluster source, and no plaintext 5672 listener exists on any node\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"RabbitMQ cluster running across node1, node2, node3\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-3175d4d5bcc54380\",\"cmd-31bcf76145f14c87\",\"cmd-ca881700da604891\"],\"summary\":\"rabbitmqctl cluster_status on node1 shows Running Nodes rabbit@node1/rabbit@node2/rabbit@node3 with no network partitions, both mid-task and after all restarts\"},{\"requirement\":\"AMQP served over TLS using a CA created for this cluster; only clients presenting a cert issued by that CA are accepted\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-d277741d14cd4ad6\",\"cmd-7e3a753c591e4de0\",\"cmd-09dfa44aae9f4f46\",\"cmd-b42c58431ccc4963\",\"cmd-b3d0ad00f0ec4a8d\",\"cmd-951c29d4baad4df6\"],\"summary\":\"CA generated on node1 (ca_cert.pem/ca_key.pem); server and client certs issued and openssl-verified against it; openssl s_client and a Python/pika end-to-end AMQP publish+consume test show TLS connections succeed only with the CA-issued client cert and fail with a 'certificate required' alert otherwise, confirmed on node1 initially and node3 post-restart\"},{\"requirement\":\"Plaintext (non-TLS) AMQP is not exposed, only TLS\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-c3bbf2453398461d\",\"cmd-8fedb56429e541b3\",\"cmd-951c29d4baad4df6\"],\"summary\":\"netstat shows no listener on 5672 on node1 and confirms 0 plaintext AMQP listeners on node2/node3 after restarts; only 5671 (TLS) is listening\"},{\"requirement\":\"Management interface reachable only from within the cluster\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-bc471648c37042ac\",\"cmd-6a05089afb5d4e81\",\"cmd-0280a0b5a2de4bc3\",\"cmd-734f6f040a3c46c7\",\"cmd-881e709e38df4fc2\",\"cmd-ca881700da604891\"],\"summary\":\"RMQ_MGMT iptables chain allowing only the three cluster node IPs and dropping all else is installed and persisted on every node; curl from an allowed cluster IP reaches the API (200/401 depending on auth) while curl from a non-listed source (127.0.0.1) times out/drops, verified both mid-task and after final node1 restart; cross-node access (node2 -> node1 management) also confirmed reachab
...[TRUNCATED 862 CHARACTERS]...
bbitMQ app back up under the correct node identity after each restart\"},{\"requirement\":\"Cluster quorum/health maintained through the restart sequence, one node at a time\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-aa16556fc7db4ee8\",\"cmd-18054e8427c14334\",\"cmd-9e7e0b00a613451a\",\"cmd-31bcf76145f14c87\",\"cmd-ca881700da604891\"],\"summary\":\"cluster_status polled after each individual node restart shows all three nodes back in Running Nodes with no partitions before the next node was restarted\"}]}","structured_output":{"status":"completed","summary":"Deployed a 3-node RabbitMQ 4.2.7 cluster (node1/node2/node3, Alpine 3.23) serving AMQP exclusively over TLS on port 5671, secured by a purpose-built CA. Client certificate authentication is mandatory (verify_peer + fail_if_no_peer_cert=true); the plaintext 5672 listener is disabled entirely. The management UI (15672) is gated by an iptables chain (RMQ_MGMT) that only accepts traffic from the three cluster node IPs and drops everything else, persisted via rc-update/iptables-save. Ran the mandatory one-at-a-time restart protocol on all three nodes: captured baseline boot_id, rebooted, confirmed boot_id changed, and verified RabbitMQ/TLS/firewall/cluster membership fully recovered before moving to the next node, preserving quorum throughout. Along the way discovered and fixed a persistence bug: /etc/hostname was \"localhost\" (runtime hostname set out-of-band by provisioning), which caused node3's first reboot to rejoin the cluster under a spurious Erlang node name; fixed /etc/hostname on all three nodes and cleaned up the stray cluster member before node2/node1 restarts, after which both came back correctly as rabbit@node2/rabbit@node1.","actions_performed":["Installed rabbitmq-server, erlang27, openssl, iptables on all three nodes via apk","Generated a dedicated CA (ca_key.pem/ca_cert.pem) on node1","Issued CA-signed server certs (with SAN DNS+IP entries for all 3 nodes) for node1/node2/node3 and a CA-signed client cert for connection testing; verified full chain of trust with openssl verify","Distributed CA cert plus per-node server cert/key to /etc/rabbitmq/tls on each node (via a temporary SSH unlock of root, then relocked root accounts afterward) with rabbitmq:rabbitmq ownership and restrictive file permissions","Generated one shared Erlang cookie on node1 and distributed it identically (md5-verified) to node2/node3","Wrote rabbitmq.conf on all nodes: listeners.tcp = none (disables plaintext AMQP), listeners.ssl.default = 5671, ssl_options with cacertfile/certfile/keyfile, verify = verify_peer, fail_if_no_peer_cert = true, classic_config peer discovery listing all three nodes, cluster_partition_handling = pause_minority","Created and persisted (iptables-save + rc-update add iptables default) an RMQ_MGMT iptables chain on every node restricting TCP/15672 to the three cluster node IPs and dropping all other sources","Started rabbitmq-server on all three nodes and confirmed automatic cluster formation (Running Nodes: rabbit@node1/2/3, no partitions)","Enabled rabbitmq_management plugin on all nodes; created an administrator user (rmqadmin, generated password) and deleted the default guest account","Enabled rabbitmq-server and iptables in the default OpenRC runlevel on all nodes so both survive reboot","Verified TLS enforcement: openssl s_client and a Python/pika AMQP publish+consume test succeed only when a CA-issued client cert is presented; both fail with 'certificate required' TLS alert otherwise","Verified management firewall: curl to the management API succeeds (200 OK, authenticated) from a cluster node IP and is dropped/times out from a non-listed source (127.0.0.1)","Discovered /etc/hostname was 'localhost' on all nodes (runtime hostname set out-of-band); after node3's first reboot it rejoined as rabbit@vm-rhel8-... instead of rabbit@node3 — fixed /etc/hostname on all three nodes, forgot the stray cluster member, and cleaned up its mnesia state","Executed the mandatory one-node-at-a-time restart protocol on node3, node2, then node1: captured pre-restart boot_id via /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id, issued a background 'reboot' whose acknowledgement returned before disconnect, polled until reachable, confirmed boot_id changed, and confirmed RabbitMQ cluster membership, TLS listener, and management firewall all recovered before proceeding to the next node","Final full-cluster verification after all three restarts: cluster_status shows all three nodes running with no partitions, TLS client-cert enforcement still works end-to-end (publish/consume succeeds with cert, fails without), management API reachable/authenticated from cluster IP and blocked from a non-cluster source, and no plaintext 5672 listener exists on any node"],"evidence":[{"requirement":"RabbitMQ cluster running across node1, node2, node3","command_ids":["cmd-3175d4d5bcc54380","cmd-31bcf76145f14c87","cmd-ca881700da604891"],"summary":"rabbitmqctl cluster_status on node1 shows Running Nodes rabbit@node1/rabbit@node2/rabbit@node3 with no network partitions, both mid-task and after all restarts"},{"requirement":"AMQP served over TLS using a CA created for this cluster; only clients presenting a cert issued by that CA are accepted","command_ids":["cmd-d277741d14cd4ad6","cmd-7e3a753c591e4de0","cmd-09dfa44aae9f4f46","cmd-b42c58431ccc4963","cmd-b3d0ad00f0ec4a8d","cmd-951c29d4baad4df6"],"summary":"CA generated on node1 (ca_cert.pem/ca_key.pem); server and client certs issued and openssl-verified against it; openssl s_client and a Python/pika end-to-end AMQP publish+consume test show TLS connections succeed only with the CA-issued client cert and fail with a 'certificate required' alert otherwise, confirmed on node1 initially and node3 post-restart"},{"requirement":"Plaintext (non-TLS) AMQP is not exposed, only TLS","command_ids":["cmd-c3bbf2453398461d","cmd-8fedb56429e541b3","cmd-951c29d4baad4df6"],"summary":"netstat shows no listener on 5672 on node1 and confirms 0 plaintext AMQP listeners on node2/node3 after restarts; only 5671 (TLS) is listening"},{"requirement":"Management interface reachable only from within the cluster","command_ids":["cmd-bc471648c37042ac","cmd-6a05089afb5d4e81","cmd-0280a0b5a2de4bc3","cmd-734f6f040a3c46c7","cmd-881e709e38df4fc2","cmd-ca881700da604891"],"summary":"RMQ_MGMT iptables chain allowing only the three cluster node IPs and dropping all else is installed and persisted on every node; curl from an allowed cluster IP reaches the API (200/401 depending on auth) while curl from a non-listed source (127.0.0.1) times out/drops, verified both mid-task and after final node1 restart; cross-node access (node2 -> node1 management) also confirmed reachable"},{"requirement":"Mandatory node-restart evidence: healthy baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, recovered state per node","command_ids":["cmd-427a910ba2e548ba","cmd-2e4ce4c0ca1a4d1c","cmd-6da85dc4019c4af4","cmd-bf6770a312b94db8","cmd-fb7fdd426bb2494c","cmd-2c748191709e4db3","cmd-ed0044db40124cd2","cmd-324e543506614d6a","cmd-df0dda30a0584811","cmd-dc896d1141864ded","cmd-6502e454150540a7","cmd-9db816323de64b29"],"summary":"For each node: baseline boot_id/status captured, background 'reboot' acknowledged before disconnect, subsequent poll shows a changed /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id (node3: dcc72341->ef4c99a3->set correctly as rabbit@node3 after hostname fix; node2: c171b954->31ad0668, rejoined as rabbit@node2; node1: f4dd55b3->742964db, rejoined as rabbit@node1), with rabbitmqctl status confirming the RabbitMQ app back up under the correct node identity after each restart"},{"requirement":"Cluster quorum/health maintained through the restart sequence, one node at a time","command_ids":["cmd-aa16556fc7db4ee8","cmd-18054e8427c14334","cmd-9e7e0b00a613451a","cmd-31bcf76145f14c87","cmd-ca881700da604891"],"summary":"cluster_status polled after each individual node restart shows all three nodes back in Running Nodes with no partitions before the next node was restarted"}]},"ttft